<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  org.ruyaos.Input1 — Ruya Input: controllers, capabilities, routing, profiles (DRAFT).

  DRAFT (T-366 / RUY-2, lead 2026-11-12), partly built. ruya-inputd serves the Devices members
  ListDevices, DeviceAdded, DeviceChanged, DeviceRemoved and the Routing members SetRoute, Route,
  RouteChanged since RUY-3 (T-367), GetCapabilities and the button test (StartTest, StopTest,
  TestInput, TestEnded) since RUY-39 (T-368), and the controller profile members (ListProfiles,
  GetProfile, SaveProfile, ValidateProfile, DeleteProfile, AssignProfile for the global scope,
  GetEffectiveProfile, ProfilesChanged) since RUY-42 (T-369); every other member below is still
  DRAFT and not served (UI ownership T-397..T-399, platform and game assignments T-370 / RUY-43,
  OwnershipMode T-371). The design is docs/specs/06-input.md. Each task may change what it builds
  here (contracts are drafts until the first release, root CLAUDE.md); RUY-403's served-vs-XML
  test must check only the built members.

  Served by ruya-inputd (system user ruya-input, not root: 06 §3) on the system bus at
  /org/ruyaos/Input1. Bus policy: ruya-input owns the name; only ruya-shell and root may call.
  Internal (tier A, ADR-022): apps never call it. They see input only as evdev virtual devices of
  their own profile (docs/developers/controller-input.md) and, later, semantic actions through
  Platform1 (T-374).

  Trust: the `profile` argument of SetRoute is trusted (the shell passes the active profile, as with
  Session1.Launch); the service refuses accounts that are not profile accounts ("unknown-profile").
  When the caller that set the current route leaves the bus, the route falls back to "locked".

  Status strings are stable API:
    "ok"               success
    "unknown-device"   no connected device with that id
    "unknown-profile"  no such console profile (SetRoute, and every profile member: the console
                       profile must be a profile account, as for SetRoute), or no such controller
                       profile
    "unknown-app"      not an installed app id (assignments, ownership modes)
    "invalid"          malformed argument, or a controller profile the schema or the service's rules
                       refuse (errors says why), or a parent (`inherits`) that does not exist
    "not-allowed"      the action does not fit the current state (a button test while an app is in
                       front, saving over or deleting a built-in profile, saving a profile of scope
                       "builtin", a cycle in `inherits`, a chain of more than 8 `inherits` links,
                       more than 64 stored profiles of one console profile)
    "busy"             a button test already runs for that device
    "failed"           internal error (also: the profiles could not be read or written)

  Records are a{sv} so fields can be added later (unknown keys are ignored).

  Device record (a{sv}), keys:
    id          s   stable id, "<bus>-<vid>-<pid>-<key>" (06 §4.1; never an event node, no raw MAC)
    instance    s   "<id>#<n>", one per connection
    name        s   the driver's name ("DualSense Wireless Controller")
    family      s   "xbox" | "playstation" | "nintendo" | "generic"
    connection  s   "usb" | "bluetooth" | "receiver" | "virtual"
    model       s   the controller model whose silhouette the shell draws: the file stem of a drawing in
                    shell/art/pads/ ("dualsense", "wiiu_pro", ...), "" when the table does not know the
                    pad (the shell then draws the family's). From the USB ids and the driver's name;
                    the same table as Devices1's `model` (services/ruya-pads; RUY-491; a missing key
                    is "")
    layout      s   how the Button test draws the pad: "nintendo-wiiu" (Wii U Pro, 057e:0330), or ""
                    for the family's default drawing (RUY-486; a missing key is "")
    battery     i   0..100, or -1 when unknown or not on battery
    charging    b
    slot        u   ADR-017 player slot, 0 when none (T-264 fills it)
    profile     s   the console profile that owns the pad ("" when none); until T-264 every pad
                    is the route's profile's
    routed      b   the pad has a virtual pad for that profile and its node's ACL is set (after
                    udev's last event on it): an app of the profile can open it now

  Capability (a(sbbbb)): name, hardware, driver, available, output (06 §4.3). Names:
    buttons sticks analog_triggers dpad gyro accelerometer touchpad rumble trigger_rumble
    adaptive_triggers lightbar player_leds speaker microphone battery
  GetCapabilities lists every name, in this order, as found when the device connected; hardware
  false means the model does not have it. Nothing is inferred (rumble never implies
  adaptive_triggers); a missing node is neither driver nor available (services/ruya-inputd/src/caps.rs).

  Routes: "shell" | "app" | "overlay" | "locked" (06 §5.1). Ownership modes: "managed" |
  "passthrough" | "steam" | "semantic" (06 §5.3).

  Controller profiles are JSON text, schema docs/specs/schemas/input-profile-v1.json (06 §7; the
  format is public, docs/developers/controller-input.md). The service validates every profile on
  load, save and import: the schema, plus the rules it cannot express (64 KiB, macro limits, button
  and axis names, dead zones that leave a range, no self-inheritance, plain-text names;
  services/ruya-inputd/src/profile.rs). Errors are "path: message" lines ("/mappings/0/to/code:
  "jump" is not a button"); they are for the journal and never shown to the player as they are.
  Built-in profiles: "default" (every input to itself, the default stick settings) and "nintendo"
  (south and east, west and north swapped; inherits "default"). A profile without inherits
  inherits from "default". Stored per console profile in /var/lib/ruya-input/profiles/<profile>/
  (0700, owned by ruya-input).
  Profile summary (a{sv}), keys:
    id                  s   the profile id
    name                s
    scope               s   "builtin" | "global" | "platform" | "game"
    scope_id            s   the app id for platform and game, else ""
    inherits            s   its parent as the file says ("" means "default")
    builtin             b
    assigned            b   the console profile's global assignment of its global_slot names it
    global_slot         s   the AssignProfile "global" scope_id its match names (RUY-545): "device:<id>"
                            when it has match.device, else "family:<f>" with match.family, else ""
                            (all controllers; every built-in)
    match_family        s   its own match.family, "" when none
    match_device        s   its own match.device (a stable device id), "" when none
    layout              s   "nintendo" when its chain holds the built-in "nintendo", else "standard"
    remapped            u   buttons it maps differently from its parent (the "changed" count of a
                            remap list on top of a layout)
    left_stick_changed  b   the resolved left stick settings differ from the built-in default's
    right_stick_changed b   the same for the right stick
    changed             b   the resolved profile maps or sets anything differently from "default"
  ListProfiles lists the built-ins first, then the stored profiles by id.
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.ruyaos.Input1">

    <!-- Devices (T-367 built: ListDevices and the three signals; GetCapabilities built, T-368).
         Only physical pads are listed, never the service's own virtual devices. -->
    <method name="ListDevices">
      <arg name="devices" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="GetCapabilities">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="capabilities" type="a(sbbbb)" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="DeviceAdded">
      <arg name="device" type="a{sv}"/>
    </signal>
    <!-- Battery, slot, owner or capabilities changed. -->
    <signal name="DeviceChanged">
      <arg name="device" type="a{sv}"/>
    </signal>
    <signal name="DeviceRemoved">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Live button test (T-368, built). StartTest: "ok", "unknown-device", "not-allowed" (the
         route is not "shell"), "busy" (a test already runs for that device). StopTest: "ok" (also
         when no test runs), "unknown-device". A test ends by StopTest, by a route other than
         "shell", when its pad disconnects, when its caller leaves the bus, or 120 s after the
         pad's last input; each end is one TestEnded.
         TestInput, while a test runs, for every input of that pad:
           input   the normalised name (06 §4.2: south east west north l1 r1 select start guide
                   l3 r3 dpad_up dpad_down dpad_left dpad_right, left_x left_y right_x right_y
                   left_trigger right_trigger), or, for an input without a normal name, its
                   raw source
           value   a button 1 (pressed) or 0; an axis its normalised value (sticks -32768..32767,
                   up negative; triggers 0..32767); a raw input the kernel's value
           source  the raw source, "raw:<node>:<type>:<code>" ("raw:pad:1:304"; node "pad" is the
                   pad's main event node)
         Buttons are sent at once; each axis at most 30 times a second, its newest value always
         arriving (held back at most 1/30 s).
         TestEnded reason: "stopped" | "idle" | "route" | "gone" | "caller".
         StartTest hold_left_stick (RUY-479): true holds that pad's left stick out of navigation
         while this test runs: its arrow keys are not sent (one already down is released at
         once), TestInput still reports it; the D-pad and the buttons keep navigating. When the
         test ends with the stick pushed it sends no key until it has been near neutral. false
         changes nothing. -->
    <method name="StartTest">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="hold_left_stick" type="b" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="StopTest">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="TestInput">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="input" type="s"/>
      <arg name="value" type="i"/>
      <arg name="source" type="s"/>
    </signal>
    <signal name="TestEnded">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="reason" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Routing (T-367, built). The shell calls it on every focus, overlay, sign-in, switch, lock
         and freeze change (06 §5.1-5.2; RUY-409, shell/qml/InputRoute.qml), and again whenever the
         name gets a new owner (a restarted service starts in "shell").
           route     "shell" | "app" | "overlay" | "locked"
           instance  Session1's instance of the app in front for "app" and "overlay" (opaque: never
                     parsed), else ""; "overlay" with "" is an overlay over a frozen app
           app_id    the Flatpak id of that app (the service picks the game or platform profile by
                     it, RUY-43), "" exactly when instance is ""; only its form is checked (the
                     rule of Session1: three dot-separated parts or more), it is not looked up
           profile   the active console profile id ("p1"), "" only for "shell" before a sign-in
                     and always for "locked"
         Status: "ok", "invalid" (unknown route, a malformed or missing instance or app_id, a
         profile for "locked"), "unknown-profile" (not a profile account: root, system users and
         ruya-shell never are). Virtual pads exist only for "app", and stay (held in neutral) for
         "overlay" with the same instance and profile; anything else destroys them, so an app's
         open descriptor reads ENODEV. When the caller that set the route leaves the bus, the route
         becomes "locked". At start the route is "shell" with no profile. -->
    <method name="SetRoute">
      <arg name="route" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <property name="Route" type="s" access="read"/>
    <signal name="RouteChanged">
      <arg name="route" type="s"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- UI ownership and the active input (T-397..T-399, 06 §11). A foreign pad's press while a
         profile is signed in: `kind` is "press" (first press per pad and session, for the toast),
         "guide" (switch / Who's playing) or "takeover" (every owner pad is gone). -->
    <signal name="ForeignInput">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="kind" type="s"/>
    </signal>
    <!-- Make a pad an owner pad of the console profile ("" releases it). -->
    <method name="AssignPad">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- The device of the last press (owner pads once T-264 has them: today every pad, 06 §11.4);
         family as in the device record. Sent when the device differs from the last announced one,
         not on every press. The keyboard is the shell's own knowledge, not reported here. -->
    <signal name="ActiveInputChanged">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="family" type="s"/>
    </signal>
    <!-- mode: "family" (as on the controller, default) | "south" | "east" (06 §11.2). Per console
         profile (`profile`, which must be a profile account), kept in ruya-input's state directory
         and applied from the next press on; a button held now is released as it was pressed.
         It changes the shell's navigation keys only (accept and back); the virtual pads stay
         positional. Status: "ok", "invalid" (not one of the modes), "unknown-profile" (not a
         profile account), "failed" (not written; nothing changed). -->
    <method name="SetConfirmButton">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- ConfirmButton: the mode of the signed-in profile (the route's), "family" with none; it
         changes with PropertiesChanged on SetConfirmButton for that profile and on a profile
         switch. -->
    <property name="ConfirmButton" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!-- Controller profiles (T-369 built; platform and game assignments T-370), stored per console
         profile (`profile`, which must be a profile account, else "unknown-profile"). Every change
         (save, delete, assign) is one ProfilesChanged and reaches the virtual pads of that console
         profile at once: what each pad emits is the global profile that applies to it (RUY-545,
         06 §7): the one assigned to its device's slot, else its family's, else all controllers',
         else "default". A held button is released and its new target pressed. -->
    <method name="ListProfiles">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="profiles" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="GetProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- GetProfile: the profile as stored (only what it sets; a built-in as the console has it);
         "unknown-profile" for an id that is neither.
         SaveProfile creates or replaces (same id). Import is ValidateProfile, then SaveProfile.
         Status "ok" (profile_id is the id), "invalid" (errors say why: the file, or a parent that
         does not exist), "not-allowed" (a built-in id or scope, a cycle, more than 8 links for it or
         for a profile that inherits from it, a 65th profile), "failed" (not written; nothing
         changed). The stored text is the service's own writing of the profile (keys sorted, whole
         numbers without a fraction), not the caller's text. -->
    <method name="SaveProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="errors" type="as" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- The file alone: "ok", or "invalid" with errors; its parent is checked by SaveProfile. -->
    <method name="ValidateProfile">
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="errors" type="as" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- "ok", "unknown-profile", "not-allowed" (a built-in). Its children inherit from its parent
         afterwards and keep their overrides; a global assignment that named it is removed. -->
    <method name="DeleteProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- scope: "global" | "platform" | "game" (scope_id: the app id). Built today: "global" (RUY-545
         slots): a console profile has up to one global profile per slot, scope_id "" (all
         controllers), "family:<f>" (f one of xbox, playstation, nintendo, generic) or "device:<id>"
         (a stable device id); a pad takes its device's slot, else its family's, else "", else
         "default". The profile is a built-in or a global profile whose global_slot (its match) is
         this scope_id; "invalid" for another slot, a malformed scope_id, or a profile of scope
         platform or game; "unknown-profile" for an id that does not exist. profile_id "" empties
         that slot only; assigning one slot never touches another. A SaveProfile that changes an
         assigned profile's match takes it out of its old slot. "platform" and "game" answer
         "invalid" until T-370 / RUY-43. ListProfiles' assigned and global_slot read the slots. -->
    <method name="AssignProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="scope" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="scope_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- What would apply to this device in this app: the resolved profile and its chain,
         most specific first (game, platform, global, builtin). json is one schema-v1 document: the
         most specific profile's id, name, scope and match, no inherits, every mapping of the
         chain, and both sticks' settings complete (the defaults filled in). device_id "" is a pad
         that no match names; a device_id that is not connected is "unknown-device". app_id is ""
         or an app id ("invalid" otherwise); until T-370 / RUY-43 it does not change the answer. -->
    <method name="GetEffectiveProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="chain" type="as" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="ProfilesChanged">
      <arg name="profile" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Ownership modes (T-371), per app; applied at the next launch. -->
    <method name="GetOwnershipMode">
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="SetOwnershipMode">
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

  </interface>
</node>
